# Indian Art and Culture: Temples, Stupas, Dances, and Music

> Beyond kings and battles, India's past lives most vividly in its
> art — the stooping Buddha of Sarnath, the soaring gopurams of the
> south, the stone rhythms of Bharatanatyam. This lesson tours the
> classical arts every exam and every traveller should recognise.

## 1. Architecture I: Stupas, Chaityas, and Viharas

[definition]
The **stupa** — a hemispherical burial mound (Sanskrit *stūpa*:
"heap") housing relics of the Buddha or monks — is earliest Buddhist
architecture: a dome (*anda*), a square railing (*harmika*), a
parasol (*chhatra*), around which devotees walk clockwise
(*pradakshina*).
[/definition]

| Stupa | Built / enlarged | Distinctive |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Sanchi (MP)** | Ashoka; enlarged by Shungas, Satavahanas | Four carved toranas; UNESCO site |
| **Bharhut (MP)** | Shunga era | Railing sculptures now in Kolkata museum |
| **Amaravati (AP)** | Satavahana era | Marble-like limestone reliefs |
| **Dhamek Stupa, Sarnath** | Gupta brick-core | Where the first sermon was preached |
| **Borobudur (Java)** | Shailendra dynasty, 8th c. | World's largest, terraced mandala |

Cave architecture completes the Buddhist trio: **chaitya** (prayer
hall with stupa, apsidal, barrel-vaulted — Karle, Bhaja, Ajanta's
chaitya) and **vihara** (monastery cells around a courtyard — Nalanda
is the grand scale-up). Jaina and Brahmanical caves follow:
**Ellora** (all three faiths, incl. the monolithic Kailasa temple),
**Elephanta** (Shiva Mahadeva), **Badami** (Chalukya).

## 2. Architecture II: The Temple — Nagara, Dravida, Vesara

@diagram tree id="temple-styles" layout="tree" aria="Indian temple styles"
  "Temple Styles" -> "Nagara (North)"
  "Temple Styles" -> "Dravida (South)"
  "Temple Styles" -> "Vesara (Deccan)"
  "Nagara (North)" -> "Curvilinear shikhara"
  "Nagara (North)" -> "No boundary walls/gopuram"
  "Dravida (South)" -> "Pyramid vimana"
  "Dravida (South)" -> "Gopuram gateways + tanks"
  "Vesara (Deccan)" -> "Star-shaped / hybrid plans"
@/diagram

| Style | Signature | Landmark temples |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Nagara** | Curvilinear *shikhara*, *amalaka* + *kalasha* finial; *panchayatana* plan; no enclosure | Khajuraho (Chandella — Kandariya Mahadeva), Konark Sun (Ganga — ratha chariot), Somnath, Modhera |
| **Dravida** | Stepped-pyramid *vimana*, towering *gopurams*, water tank, boundary wall | Brihadeshvara (Rajaraja I, Thanjavur), Shore Temple Mahabalipuram, Meenakshi Madurai, Rameshwaram |
| **Vesara** | Stellate (star) plans; fusion | Hoysala: Belur Chennakeshava, Halebidu, Somnathpura (Karnataka); Pattadakal (UNESCO) mixes both |

[tip]
Memory hooks: Nagara = **North** (no walls, beehive tower);
Dravida = **Deccan & Tamil** (city-gate gopurams, temple tank);
Vesara = **Karnataka's star platforms** (Hoysala). Rock-cut phase:
Mahishamardini cave & rathas at Mahabalipuram (Pallava); Kailasa at
Ellora (Rashtrakuta Krishna I) carved top-down from one rock.
[/tip]

## 3. Sculpture: The Classical Idols

- **Harappan**: bronze "Dancing Girl" and the steatite
  **Priest-King** (Mohenjo-daro) — the deep prelude.
- **Mauryan**: polished **Chunar sandstone** monoliths — the lion
  capital of Sarnath (our national emblem), the Didarganj Yakshi, the
  rampart-walking lions at Vaishali.
- **Gandhara** (NW, 1st–5th c. CE): Greco-Roman realism — wavy hair,
  muscular folds, grey schist Buddhas.
- **Mathura** (UP): red mottled sandstone; indigenous, robust —
  seated Buddha of Sarnath's first sermon; earliest Jina images.
- **Amaravati** (AP): white limestone, supple narrative relief.
- **Gupta (classical golden standard)**: Sarnath Buddha in
  *dharma-chakra-pravartana mudra* — serene, transparent drapery;
  Sultanganj bronze Buddha (2.3 m); Udayagiri caves.

## 4. Classical Dances: The Eight Recognised

| Dance | State | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **Bharatanatyam** | Tamil Nadu | Temple tradition of devadasis; revived by Rukmini Devi Arundale; Sadir roots |
| **Kathak** | UP / North | Mughal courts; footwork, spins, storytelling (*kathakars*) |
| **Kathakali** | Kerala | Green-painted faces (*pacha*), elaborate mudras, all-male, night-long epics |
| **Mohiniyattam** | Kerala | "Dance of the enchantress," lasya — graceful, white-gold costume |
| **Kuchipudi** | Andhra Pradesh | Bhama Kalapam; dance-drama on a brass plate |
| **Odissi** | Odisha | Devadasi of Jagannath Puri; *tribhanga* pose; held in temple reliefs |
| **Manipuri** | Manipur | Radha-Krishna *ras*; veiled women in stiff skirt (kumil) |
| **Sattriya** | Assam | Sankardeva's *satra* monasteries, 15th c. — newest recognised (2000) |

The grammar of them all: **Natya Shastra** of Bharata (c. 200 BCE–200
CE) — the fifth Veda of dramaturgy — codifying 108 **karanas**
(dance units), mudras (hand gestures), and the theory of **rasa**
(aesthetic flavour).

## 5. Music: The Two Systems

- **Hindustani (North)**: shaped in Sultanate-Mughal courts;
  **gharanas** (Gwalior, Agra, Jaipur…); forms — *dhrupad, khayal,
  tarana, thumri*; instruments — sitar, sarod, tabla, sarangi; maestros
  — Tansen (Akbar's navaratna), later Amir Khan, Bhimsen Joshi;
  *guru-shishya parampara*.
- **Carnatic (South)**: devotional core; composed **kritis** — the
  trinity **Tyagaraja, Muthuswami Dikshitar, Syama Sastri**; ragam +
  talam + improvisation (*alapana, kalpana swara*); instruments —
  veena, violin, mridangam.

Both share **raga** (melody framework) and **tala** (rhythmic cycle)
and treat music as *nada brahma* — sound as the divine.

## 6. Painting and Other Arts

- **Murals**: Ajanta's graceful Bodhisattva Padmapani; Badami;
  Sittanavasal (Jaina); Lepakshi.
- **Miniatures**: Palas (earliest palm-leaf), Mughal atelier (Akbar's
  *Hamzanama*; Ustad Mansur's birds), Rajasthani schools (Mewar,
  Kishangarh's *Bani Thani*), Pahari (Kangra's Krishna themes), and
  the narrative-scroll **Phad** of Rajasthan; **Madhubani (Mithila)**
  folk art; **Pattachitra** of Odisha.
- **Other classical arts**: Koodiyattam and Kutiyattam (Sanskrit
  drama, UNESCO), Vedic recitation (UNESCO), Kalamkari cloth,
  Bidriware, Chola bronzes (Nataraja's *ananda-tandava*).

[summary]
Indian classical culture rests on a shared grammar: Bharata's Natya
Shastra and its rasa theory. Architecture spans Buddhist stupas
(Sanchi), chaitya-vihara caves (Karle, Ajanta), and three temple
languages — Nagara's curvilinear shikharas (Khajuraho), Dravida's
gopuram cities (Brihadeshvara), Vesara's star platforms (Hoysala).
Sculpture runs Gandhara's realism, Mathura's vigour, and the Gupta
serenity of the Sarnath Buddha. Eight classical dances — from
Bharatanatyam to Sattriya — and twin music systems of Hindustani and
Carnatic, united by raga and tala, complete the classical arts.
[/summary]
